WEBVTT

00:00:00.560 --> 00:00:06.080
Hi, this is Dr. Jelena Trajkovic from the CECS Department at Cal State Long Beach. It is important

00:00:06.080 --> 00:00:17.070
to note that so far we have thought about strings of zeros and ones and we were thinking of them

00:00:17.070 --> 00:00:27.910
always as representations of numbers. Binary information can represent something else.

00:00:27.910 --> 00:00:34.290
So first and foremost, let's start with a different representation of numbers, which

00:00:34.290 --> 00:00:36.490
is called BCD.

00:00:36.490 --> 00:00:41.650
So basically this discrete set of zeros and numbers can be associated to something else

00:00:41.650 --> 00:00:43.410
in a different way.

00:00:43.410 --> 00:00:54.910
BCD codes have been developed because we as humans are really used to decimal representation.

00:00:54.910 --> 00:01:09.660
So what is happening in BCD? We have taken one digit, one digit in decimal. In each digit

00:01:09.660 --> 00:01:27.580
decimal is represented as four different digits binary because so five is zero one zero one

00:01:28.780 --> 00:01:37.180
we represent it that way so basically we partition our decimal in ones tens hundred and so and so

00:01:37.180 --> 00:01:44.420
so forth, and each of those is going to be used, is going to be represented using separate

00:01:44.420 --> 00:02:00.060
subset of four binary digits. So one is going to be represented as 001. Why four? Because

00:02:00.060 --> 00:02:11.610
order to represent a total of 10 digits, 10 different combinations, we have to have at

00:02:11.610 --> 00:02:26.920
least 4 bits. 3 bits is not enough. So if we compare this to regular binary code, 15

00:02:26.920 --> 00:02:33.520
is going to be represented only with four bits, 1111.

00:02:33.600 --> 00:02:41.350
Sometimes BCD codes are actually easier to use

00:02:41.950 --> 00:02:46.230
and make sense, not always in the computer.

00:02:46.230 --> 00:02:50.670
And in our class, we are going to look into example

00:02:50.670 --> 00:02:56.020
of how to add BCD digital.

00:02:56.020 --> 00:03:00.080
There are also other codes.

00:03:00.080 --> 00:03:04.200
So what we want to do is we want to take a set

00:03:04.200 --> 00:03:09.200
of 10 digits that represent entire set of decimal digits.

00:03:12.680 --> 00:03:15.960
And we want to represent each of them

00:03:15.960 --> 00:03:20.960
using one subset of four digits.

00:03:21.480 --> 00:03:24.800
So as we saw previously, BCD is such

00:03:24.800 --> 00:03:27.780
that we have zero is binary zero,

00:03:27.780 --> 00:03:30.740
one is binary one, and so on and so forth.

00:03:30.740 --> 00:03:35.740
Mind you, in any of these representations,

00:03:36.240 --> 00:03:44.000
we will have digits that are going to be unused.

00:03:44.000 --> 00:03:49.000
Another example of codes are two, four, two, one.

00:03:51.020 --> 00:03:52.600
This one is funny.

00:03:52.600 --> 00:03:57.600
This position here is going to be weighed as one.

00:03:57.600 --> 00:04:06.080
1. This position here is also going to be weighed as 2. The position here is going

00:04:06.080 --> 00:04:10.480
to be weighed as 4 and believe it or not the most significant bit is going to be

00:04:10.480 --> 00:04:25.400
weighed as 2. So if we look at the example here 0 0 1 1 that example is

00:04:25.400 --> 00:04:36.280
going to be 0 times 2 plus 0 times 4 plus 1 times 2 plus 1 times 1 which is going to be 3.

00:04:37.320 --> 00:04:46.360
Let's look at something that is a little bit more exotic. So we have 1 1 0 1 because this one

00:04:46.360 --> 00:04:53.000
pretty much resembles the binary code, regular binary code. So this one is going to be 1 times 2

00:04:53.000 --> 00:05:08.390
plus one times four plus zero times two plus one times one and that is and that

00:05:08.390 --> 00:05:26.580
is two plus four plus one equals two plus two seven. Another interesting code to talk

00:05:26.580 --> 00:05:41.170
about is XS3 code. XS3 code is very similar to BCD code. However, 0 is represented as

00:05:41.170 --> 00:05:50.490
0 plus 3. So we start here. 0 is represented like 3. 1 is represented like binary 4. 2

00:05:50.490 --> 00:05:56.300
who is represented at binary five, so and so forth.

00:05:56.300 --> 00:06:01.140
Other possibility here is a funny code

00:06:01.140 --> 00:06:03.460
that has negative coefficients.

00:06:03.460 --> 00:06:09.700
So we have eight, four, minus two, and one.

00:06:09.700 --> 00:06:14.700
So let's see what does this number represent.

00:06:15.140 --> 00:06:20.140
One, zero, one, one in eight,

00:06:20.140 --> 00:06:23.840
four, minus two, minus one code

00:06:23.840 --> 00:06:28.840
is one times eight plus zero times four

00:06:31.570 --> 00:06:36.570
plus one times minus two plus one times minus one.

00:06:41.010 --> 00:06:46.010
That is eight minus two minus one plus two plus five.

00:06:51.070 --> 00:06:55.670
The code that is going to be pertinent for us

00:06:55.670 --> 00:06:57.910
and we will be using it actually

00:06:57.910 --> 00:07:12.150
one of the latest slides is gray code. Gray code came up because of necessity so let me give you

00:07:12.150 --> 00:07:21.030
the brief introduction. When you think about counting so let's count from zero sorry zero

00:07:21.030 --> 00:07:33.150
to 1, to 2, and we can keep counting more and more. But when you look at this, between

00:07:33.150 --> 00:07:42.860
this number and this number, we have only one digit that changes. However, between this

00:07:42.860 --> 00:07:56.550
number and this number, there are two digits that change. Why is that important? In our

00:07:56.550 --> 00:08:05.030
computing systems, digital systems, every time that bit changes its value from 1 to 0 or 0 to 1,

00:08:05.750 --> 00:08:13.590
we consume power. That is called dynamic power. So for purposes where we want to keep counting

00:08:15.670 --> 00:08:21.430
constantly, we want to consume less and less power. Therefore,

00:08:21.430 --> 00:08:34.930
Therefore, the grey code has been invented such that between two consecutive counts, only one digit changes.

00:08:34.930 --> 00:08:48.680
Okay, we go from 0 to 1, one digit changes. From 1 to 2, we have another zero digit that changes.

00:08:48.680 --> 00:08:55.840
from 2 to 3, again only one value of one

00:08:55.840 --> 00:09:03.740
digit is going to change. 3 to 4, again

00:09:03.740 --> 00:09:06.260
only one single digit is going to

00:09:06.260 --> 00:09:13.620
change. And that's how we that's how we

00:09:13.620 --> 00:09:21.860
benefit from gray code. It's very very

00:09:21.860 --> 00:09:30.520
common to be used for representation of analog signals because analog signals

00:09:30.520 --> 00:09:38.480
are going to continuously change in time. So counters are one

00:09:38.480 --> 00:09:46.320
example and the other examples are analog signals because consecutive values

00:09:46.320 --> 00:09:51.700
are very likely to be, that are measured in analog, are very likely to be numbers

00:09:51.700 --> 00:09:54.900
that are next to each other consecutive binary.

00:09:56.020 --> 00:09:57.380
Pay attention to gray code.

00:09:57.380 --> 00:10:00.340
Our last lab, if I remember correctly,

00:10:00.340 --> 00:10:01.620
is going to use gray codes.

00:10:02.420 --> 00:10:04.500
Please let me know if you have any questions.

